# Line endings are normalised on the way INTO git, not in your working tree.
#
# This repo is edited on Windows and deployed on Linux. Without this file, a
# `git add -A` from a Windows checkout rewrites every file it touches with CRLF
# -- 348 of them, at the time this was added, against 12 with real changes. The
# diff becomes unreadable, and worse: `setup.sh` with CRLF endings does not run
# on Linux at all. It fails on the first function definition with
#
#   syntax error near unexpected token `$'{\r''
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` stores everything as LF regardless of how it looks locally.
# Windows editors keep showing CRLF; git simply stops recording it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts are the case where this is not cosmetic -- see above.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Never touch these.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.nemo binary
*.pt   binary
*.onnx binary
*.tgz  binary
*.gz   binary
